From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] shared/tinyusb: Ctrl-C reaches a program behind unread
 console input.

On a native USB console, a running program that doesn't read stdin could
not be interrupted once 260 characters were waiting (omnipython-builds#575).
tud_cdc_rx_cb() moves a byte from the USB FIFO to the stdin ring only
while the ring has room, and looks for the interrupt character only on the
bytes it moves. With the ring full, the Ctrl-C stayed in the FIFO behind
the earlier characters, and the program ran on until it ended or the board
was reset.

While the interrupt character is set, which is while a program runs with
Ctrl-C enabled, a byte that finds the ring full is now read and dropped,
and the interrupt character still raises KeyboardInterrupt. A UART console
does the same when its ring is full. At the REPL, and in a program that
has called micropython.kbd_intr(-1) to read raw bytes, the interrupt
character is unset and the host is held back as before, so nothing is
lost there.

On a QT Py ESP32-S3, with 270, 300, 600 and 2000 characters typed into a
running time.sleep(20), Ctrl-C was ignored before and now interrupts in
0.2 s, as it does with fewer. A program under kbd_intr(-1) reading 3000
bytes slowly still gets all of them, a 2000-character line pasted at the
REPL arrives whole, and a 30 KB file put over the raw REPL verifies.

Based-on: micropython b70ae1aca (v1.30.0-preview-160)
---
 shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c
--- a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c
+++ b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_cdc.c
@@ -92,6 +92,19 @@
             #else
             ringbuf_put(&stdin_ringbuf, data_char);
             #endif
+        #if MICROPY_KBD_EXCEPTION
+        } else if (mp_interrupt_char != -1) {
+            // The ring is full and a program is running that doesn't read
+            // it. Holding the rest back in the USB FIFO would hide the
+            // interrupt character behind it, so drop characters instead,
+            // as a UART console does when its ring is full, and still act
+            // on the interrupt character. With kbd_intr(-1), and at the
+            // REPL, the host is held back and nothing is lost.
+            if (tud_cdc_n_read_char(itf) == mp_interrupt_char) {
+                stdin_ringbuf.iget = stdin_ringbuf.iput = 0;
+                mp_sched_keyboard_interrupt();
+            }
+        #endif
         } else {
             cdc_itf_pending |= (1 << itf);
             return;
-- 
